state_arm_resetting:kgt,ui_state_program_mode:xgt,ui_state_fully_charged:Sgt,ui_state_offline:Cgt,ui_live_track:Egt,ui_live_track_driven:Tgt,ui_live_track_mopped:Agt,"IP address or interface name (for example eth0) the UDP discovery socket binds to. Empty means all interfaces, which can fail on hosts with several networks.":"IP address or interface name (for example eth0) the UDP discovery socket binds to. Empty means all interfaces, which can fail on hosts with several networks.",clean_mode_tab:zgt,set_clean_motor_mode:Igt,"Map Colour Scheme":"Map Colour Scheme","Follow the admin theme":"Follow the admin theme","Room colours only. The floor, the walls and the driven path are set by the map colour scheme below.":"Room colours only. The floor, the walls and the driven path are set by the map colour scheme below.","Floor, walls and driven path of the rendered map. 'Light' is the picture the adapter has always produced. 'Follow the admin theme' takes the theme the Roborock tab reports; since there is only one rendered image per robot, the browser that reported last decides what everyone sees.":"Floor, walls and driven path of the rendered map. 'Light' is the picture the adapter has always produced. 'Follow the admin theme' takes the theme the Roborock tab reports; since there is only one rendered image per robot, the browser that reported last decides what everyone sees.",washingTaskStatus:Ogt,washingMode:Mgt,isWashing:Rgt,isWashReady:Dgt,isDrying:Ngt,dryRemainTime:Pgt,ui_dock_washing:jgt,ui_dock_drying:Lgt,ui_dock_drying_remaining:Fgt,ui_history:Bgt,ui_history_details:qgt,ui_history_total_area:Wgt,ui_history_total_time:Hgt,ui_history_total_runs:Ugt,ui_history_result_finished:Vgt,ui_history_result_interrupted:Ggt,ui_history_interrupted_short:Xgt,ui_history_reason_manual:Kgt,ui_history_reason_breakpoint:Ygt,ui_history_reason_locate_failed:Jgt,ui_history_reason_unreachable:Zgt,ui_history_reason_wash_error:Qgt,ui_history_reason_dock_return_failed:eyt,ui_history_reason_cruise_completed:tyt,ui_history_reason_cruise_failed:nyt,ui_history_reason_pet_found:oyt,ui_history_reason_pet_not_found:ryt,ui_history_type_full:ayt,ui_history_type_zone:syt,ui_history_type_room:iyt,ui_history_type_quick_map:cyt,ui_history_type_cruise:lyt,ui_history_mop_washes:uyt,ui_history_empty:dyt,ui_history_no_map:pyt,ui_history_started:_yt,ui_history_type:hyt,ui_history_result:myt,ui_history_more_values:fyt,ui_history_type_pet_patrol:gyt,"Live position: pause between updates (ms)":"Live position: pause between updates (ms)",ui_settings_from:yyt,ui_settings_to:vyt,ui_settings:byt,ui_settings_dnd_off_hint:wyt,"Milliseconds the adapter waits after one answer before it asks for the robot position again. It is a pause, not a fixed rate: the next question goes out only once the previous answer has arrived, so the requests can never pile up and a slow connection simply slows the updates down instead. The default of 1500 ms comes from a measurement at a driving robot: it advances its own position only about every 3 seconds, so 95 percent of the requests in a 100 ms test returned the value they had already returned before. 1500 ms samples that roughly twice per update, which is enough not to skip a step. A shorter setting does not make the robot report more often - it only produces more requests, and the position it shows is on average about 1.5 seconds old whatever is configured here, because that is the robot's own update grid. 250 ms is the fastest allowed, kept well below the default so a model that updates faster than the measured one is not held back. Two limits apply automatically and cannot be undercut: while the robot stands still, and while it is only reachable through the Roborock cloud, the pause is at least 2 seconds. 0 switches the live position off; the robot is then only shown where the last map put it. Values from 1 to 30 are still read as the whole seconds this option used to count, so an existing setting keeps working - set a value of 250 or more to use the new unit.":"Milliseconds the adapter waits after one answer before it asks for the robot position again. It is a pause, not a fixed rate: the next question goes out only once the previous answer has arrived, so the requests can never pile up and a slow connection simply slows the updates down instead. The default of 1500 ms comes from a measurement at a driving robot: it advances its own position only about every 3 seconds, so 95 percent of the requests in a 100 ms test returned the value they had already returned before. 1500 ms samples that roughly twice per update, which is enough not to skip a step. A shorter setting does not make the robot report more often - it only produces more requests, and the position it shows is on average about 1.5 seconds old whatever is configured here, because that is the robot's own update grid. 250 ms is the fastest allowed, kept well below the default so a model that updates faster than the measured one is not held back. Two limits apply automatically and cannot be undercut: while the robot stands still, and while it is only reachable through the Roborock cloud, the pause is at least 2 seconds. 0 switches the live position off; the robot is then only shown where the last map put it. Values from 1 to 30 are still read as the whole seconds this option used to count, so an existing setting keeps working - set a value of 250 or more to use the new unit.",ui_map_zone_add:$yt,ui_map_zone_draft_hint:kyt,ui_map_zone_limit:xyt,ui_map_zone_no_go:Syt,ui_map_zone_no_mop:Cyt,ui_map_zone_save:Eyt,ui_map_zone_wall:Tyt,ui_map_zones:Ayt,ui_map_zones_hint:zyt,ui_map_zones_malformed:Iyt,ui_map_zones_no_map:Oyt,ui_map_zones_unreproducible:Myt,ui_zone_handle_rotate:Ryt,ui_map_zone_unconfirmed:Dyt,ui_map_zone_edit_hint:Nyt,"Live position: match the robot":"Live position: match the robot","Lets the adapter work out how often this particular robot advances its position, and paces the live position channel accordingly. Switch it off to use the fixed value above instead. The default of 1500 ms was measured at one robot; a robot that reports three times as fast would be held back by it, and its owner would never learn that there is a setting to change. What the adapter measures is the distance between two observations of a change, not the moment the robot wrote it, and it can never resolve anything finer than the pause it is currently running at - so a faster robot is approached in steps rather than recognised at once. Only a robot that is actually working counts, never one standing in its dock, and the result never goes below the fastest allowed value or above the two seconds a standing robot gets. What the adapter settled on is written to the log and to the read-only state map.liveTrackLearnedPause of each robot. Setting the value above to 0 switches the live position off entirely, and this option cannot re-enable it.":"Lets the adapter work out how often this particular robot advances its position, and paces the live position channel accordingly. Switch it off to use the fixed value above instead. The default of 1500 ms was measured at one robot; a robot that reports three times as fast would be held back by it, and its owner would never learn that there is a setting to change. What the adapter measures is the distance between two observations of a change, not the moment the robot wrote it, and it can never resolve anything finer than the pause it is currently running at - so a faster robot is approached in steps rather than recognised at once. Only a robot that is actually working counts, never one standing in its dock, and the result never goes below the fastest allowed value or above the two seconds a standing robot gets. What the adapter settled on is written to the log and to the read-only state map.liveTrackLearnedPause of each robot. Setting the value above to 0 switches the live position off entirely, and this option cannot re-enable it.",ui_room_name_invalid:Pyt,ui_room_rename:jyt,ui_remote:Lyt,ui_re